---
import { guide, site } from "../data/site";
import ArticleLayout from "../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";
---

<ArticleLayout
	description="What the SolidCraft desktop app (including its anonymous usage statistics), browser app, agent chat, and this website send over the network, and what stays on your computer."
	eyebrow="PROJECT / PRIVACY"
	title="Privacy"
>
	<p>Effective November 22, 2026.</p>
	<h2>Desktop app</h2>
	<p>
		SolidCraft has no accounts. Your designs stay on your computer unless you
		share them yourself. Since 0.46.0 the desktop app also sends anonymous usage
		statistics, described below. Those and the update check are the only things
		it sends over the network on its own; everything else happens only when you
		ask, such as sending a problem report, using the Assistant with an online
		model, or opening a design link.
	</p>
	<h3>Update check</h3>
	<p>
		At startup, and again once a day while it stays open, SolidCraft asks the
		GitHub API for the latest SolidCraft release. The request carries a fixed
		User-Agent and nothing else: no version number, no machine or user
		identifier, no usage data, and no cookies. GitHub sees it like any other
		visit to its API, including your IP address and the time. If you choose{" "}
		<strong>Update now</strong>, SolidCraft downloads the release files from
		GitHub releases. Turn the check off under Preferences › General › Updates,
		set <code>SOLIDCRAFT_UPDATE_CHECK=0</code>, or use the{" "}
		<code>CheckForUpdates</code>
		organization policy. Builds you compile yourself, the Flatpak, and the
		browser app never check. Details are in{" "}
		<a href={guide("updates")}>updates and privacy</a>.
	</p>
	<h3>Anonymous usage statistics</h3>
	<p>
		SolidCraft sends anonymous usage statistics so we know how many
		installations there are, how many are used each day, for how long, and which
		tools and operations people use. They are on by default. They count which
		tools and commands are used, never your designs, file names, or anything you
		type. The first start shows a notice with <strong>Settings</strong> and{" "}
		<strong>Dismiss</strong>
		buttons. It stays until you choose one of them, and nothing is counted
		before it has been shown.
	</p>
	<p>
		One report is sent per day of use. It holds the SolidCraft version, your
		operating system and processor architecture, whether it is the desktop or
		web app, the minutes in which you used the keyboard, mouse, or touch in the
		window, how many times each command changed your design or each tool was
		started, which commands failed and the kind of error (never its message),
		and how long operations and open dialogs took, in coarse time buckets. These
		are split by whether you, an assistant, or a plugin acted. It also carries a
		random installation ID, made the first time and not derived from your
		computer or account. Never sent: command parameters, design or file names,
		paths, geometry, sketches, text you type, plugin data, your name, account or
		email, hardware identifiers, locale, or settings.{" "}
		<strong>Preferences › Privacy › Show what&rsquo;s sent</strong>
		shows the exact contents of the next report.
	</p>
	<p>
		Reports go only to <code>https://metrics.solidcraft.io/v1/report</code>,
		over HTTPS and without cookies, passing through Cloudflare&rsquo;s network
		on the way. Only release builds send them, in the background, after the day
		has finished. Offline, a report waits and is deleted if it is not sent
		within 7 days. The server stores reports without IP addresses and keeps them
		for 90 days; after that it keeps only daily totals, with no installation ID
		in them. Only the SolidCraft maintainers see them. To limit floods of fake
		reports, the server keeps a keyed hash of the sender&rsquo;s address in
		memory only, changed daily and forgotten at the end of the day. Our hosting
		provider, Railway, keeps request logs that include the sender&rsquo;s IP
		address for up to 30 days; we don&rsquo;t use them.
	</p>
	<p>
		To turn them off, clear
		<strong>Preferences › Privacy › Share anonymous usage statistics</strong>
		(<strong>Settings</strong>
		on the notice opens it), set <code>DO_NOT_TRACK=1</code> or
		<code>SOLIDCRAFT_NO_TELEMETRY=1</code>, or use the
		<code>UsageStatistics</code>
		organization policy. When they are off, nothing is counted and reports still
		waiting are deleted.{" "}
		<strong>Delete my data</strong>
		in Preferences › Privacy asks the server to delete every report it holds for
		your installation ID and starts a new random ID; the daily totals stay,
		since they contain no ID. A build compiled without the
		<code>usage-stats</code>
		feature leaves all of this out. The full description is in the{" "}
		<a
			href="https://github.com/tangency-labs/solidcraft/blob/main/docs/privacy.md"
			>privacy documentation</a
		>.
	</p>
	<p>
		Crash reports are written to a folder on your computer. SolidCraft does not
		send them anywhere. <strong>Report a bug</strong> lets you review a
		screenshot and opens a draft GitHub issue in your browser; nothing is
		published until you submit it yourself.
	</p>
	<h2>Agent chat</h2>
	<p>
		Agent chat is optional and exists only in the desktop app. It talks to the
		coding agent you run on your computer, or to the OpenAI-compatible endpoint
		you configure (a local one such as Ollama or LM Studio, or a URL you
		choose). Your prompts, any files you attach, and what the agent reads from
		your design go only to that agent or endpoint. SolidCraft does not proxy or
		provide a model and does not send them anywhere else. What an agent sends on
		to its own service is governed by that service&rsquo;s terms. An
		endpoint&rsquo;s key is kept in your system keychain and sent only to that
		endpoint.
	</p>
	<h2>Browser app</h2>
	<p>
		The browser app runs in your browser. Designs stay in the browser&rsquo;s
		private storage or in files you choose to open and save; SolidCraft does not
		upload them. The app keeps a copy of itself in your browser so it works
		offline, and checks for a newer build from the site that serves it. Clearing
		the site&rsquo;s data can remove designs kept in browser storage, so
		download a copy of anything you want to keep. See the{" "}
		<a href={guide("files")}>file guide</a>.
	</p>
	<h2>This website</h2>
	<p>
		The site has no accounts, forms, advertising, or analytics scripts, and it
		sets no cookies. Fonts, images, and videos are served from the site itself.
	</p>
	<p>
		solidcraft.io is served through Cloudflare. Like any host, Cloudflare
		processes ordinary request information such as your IP address and request
		headers, and may keep logs or aggregate traffic statistics under its own{" "}
		<a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">privacy policy</a>.
	</p>
	<p>
		The home, download, and releases pages ask api.github.com for public release
		information directly from your browser, with no credentials. GitHub receives
		your IP address and ordinary request headers. The result is kept in your
		browser&rsquo;s local storage for five minutes, and the pages work if
		storage or GitHub is unavailable. SolidCore progress pages likewise fetch
		public reports from tangency-labs.github.io. Download links go straight to
		GitHub release files, and this site adds no tracking parameters. GitHub
		handles those requests under its{" "}
		<a
			href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement"
		>
			privacy statement
		</a
		>.
	</p>
	<h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
	<p>
		0.46.0 added anonymous usage statistics, on by default, with a notice at
		first start and an off switch in Preferences › Privacy. This page was
		updated after that release; it should have been updated before it. Future
		changes will be listed here with a new effective date and noted in the
		release notes.
	</p>
	<h2>Contact</h2>
	<p>
		Questions or corrections: open an issue at{" "}
		<a href={`${site.repo}/issues`}>tangency-labs/solidcraft</a>, or email{" "}
		<a href="mailto:contact@tangencylabs.io">contact@tangencylabs.io</a>.
	</p>
</ArticleLayout>
